\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces What the latent names on one benchmark frame (beside-resource anchored run, 100k steps). (a)\nobreakspace  {}The $64\times 64$ observation with Crafter's 7-pixel tiles (white) and the $16\times 16$-pixel cells of the encoder's final feature map (orange): one latent cell spans more than two tiles in each direction. (b)\nobreakspace  {}The true $9\times 7$ field; the station tiles are outlined. (c)\nobreakspace  {}The field head's prediction from the posterior latent: terrain is largely right (63\% of cells), but the station is replaced by path, with probability 0.00{} on the table tile. (d)\nobreakspace  {}The same field after 15 imagined steps from the prior, the horizon on which the actor is trained: the map has drifted toward grass (13\% of cells). (e)\nobreakspace  {}Probability of the true class per cell under the posterior. The frame is the probe frame with the most station cells in daylight; \cref  {sec:latent} gives statistics over all 116{,}235{} probed cells.\relax }}{2}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
