\newlabel{fig:example}{{1}{2}{What the latent names on one benchmark frame (beside-resource anchored run, 100k steps). (a)~The $64\times 64$ observation with Crafter's 7-pixel tiles (white) and the $16\times 16$-pixel cells of the encoder's final feature map (orange): one latent cell spans more than two tiles in each direction. (b)~The true $9\times 7$ field; the station tiles are outlined. (c)~The field head's prediction from the posterior latent: terrain is largely right (\ExamplePostAcc \% of cells), but the station is replaced by path, with probability \ExamplePostTable {} on the table tile. (d)~The same field after 15 imagined steps from the prior, the horizon on which the actor is trained: the map has drifted toward grass (\ExamplePriorAcc \% of cells). (e)~Probability of the true class per cell under the posterior. The frame is the probe frame with the most station cells in daylight; \cref {sec:latent} gives statistics over all \FieldCells {} probed cells.\relax }{figure.caption.1}{}}
